# Laravel Eloquent belongsTo relationship returns null

When iterating over a collection of `Post` models and accessing a `belongsTo` relationship like `$post->product`, you might expect to see the associated data immediately. However, if that accessor returns `null`, while calling `$post->product()` successfully retrieves the object (albeit in a way that is difficult to use directly), the issue almost always lies in how the database query was constructed and how Eloquent handles eager loading.

In your scenario, you are retrieving all posts using `Post::get()`. This method loads every row from the `posts` table into memory. At this point, no SQL queries are executed against the `products` table. When you subsequently access `$key->product`, Eloquent attempts to lazily load the relationship. It checks if the foreign key (`product_id`) exists in the loaded model; if it does, it typically doesn't hit the database unless explicitly told to fetch the data via a separate query, or it relies on a cached value that hasn't been populated yet.

The behavior where `$key->product()` returns an object that is hard to use directly usually indicates that a lazy-loaded instance has been instantiated, but its properties might not be fully resolved, or you are inadvertently relying on magic methods that don't expose the underlying data cleanly in a loop. More critically, if your code relies on accessing properties of the product within that same iteration without an explicit query, Eloquent hasn't actually fetched those records from the database yet.

The root cause here is the lack of eager loading. When you call `Post::get()`, Eloquent loads only the columns defined in the `$fillable` array of the `Post` model. It knows about the relationship via the definition in your code, but it has no reason to join the `products` table unless you explicitly ask for it.

To fix this, you must use the `with()` method before calling `get()`. This tells Eloquent to perform a single query that joins the `posts` and `products` tables using a LEFT JOIN. This ensures that every post returned includes its associated product data in memory, avoiding N+1 query problems and ensuring that `$post->product` returns a fully populated model instance immediately.

Here is how you should modify your retrieval logic:

```php
$posts = Post::with('product')->get();

foreach ($posts as $post) {
    // Now this will return the Product model directly
    $product = $post->product; 

    // You can safely access properties like this:
    echo $product->name; 
}
```

By using `Post::with('product')`, Eloquent executes a query similar to `SELECT * FROM posts LEFT JOIN products ON posts.product_id = products.id`. This populates the relationship cache for each model instance. Consequently, accessing `$post->product` in your loop returns the fully hydrated `Product` object with all its attributes accessible, matching the behavior you need without the ambiguity of lazy loading.

If you cannot change the initial query (for example, if you are passing a collection from another service), you can use `load()` on the collection itself:

```php
$posts = Post::get();
$posts->load('product');

foreach ($posts as $post) {
    $product = $post->product;
    // Use the product safely here
}
```

This approach resolves the null issue by ensuring the relationship data is fetched before you attempt to access it. Remember that accessing a `belongsTo` relationship without eager loading often results in either a null value or a lazy-loaded instance that requires an additional database hit, which defeats the purpose of efficient data retrieval in high-traffic applications. Always prefer eager loading when working with collections where relationships are accessed repeatedly.
